## DEFINITION
NCBI (National Center for Biotechnology Information) is a public bioinformatics resource that provides access to biological databases, 
sequence analysis tools, and scientific literature.

## PURPOSE
- Store and retrieve biological data.
- Provide sequence analysis tools.
- Support genomics and biomedical research.
- Integrate biological information from multiple databases.

## MAJOR DATABASES
- GenBank
- Gene
- Protein
- PubMed
- RefSeq
- SNP (dbSNP)

## COMMON USES
- Retrieve DNA, RNA, and protein sequences.
- Search scientific literature.
- Perform BLAST searches.
- Access gene and genome information.

## IMPORTANT FILE FORMATS
- FASTA (.fasta, .fa)
- GenBank (.gb, .gbk)
- ASN.1
- XML
- GFF

## STEPS FOR SEQUENCE RETRIEVAL
- Open the NCBI website.
- Select the appropriate database.
- Search using a gene name, accession number, or organism.
- Open the desired record.
- Download the sequence in the required format (e.g., FASTA or GenBank).

## OUTPUT
- DNA/RNA sequences
- Protein sequences
- Gene records
- Genome information
- Literature records (PubMed)

## FILES TO UPLOAD
- FASTA (.fasta, .fa)
- GenBank (.gb, .gbk)
- Sequence (.txt)
- Accession ID or Gene ID

## IMPORTANT POINTS
- One of the world's largest biological data repositories.
- Integrates multiple databases and analysis tools.
- Freely accessible for research and education.
- Widely used in genomics, proteomics, and molecular biology.
